WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.420
A cabin lamp has gone dark.

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:04.261
Shall we guess, or measure?

00:00:04.481 --> 00:00:07.681
Join cadet Saga and the Gull in the multimeter lab.

00:00:07.901 --> 00:00:11.720
We will practise connections, instrument selection and calculations.

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:15.503
Finally, we will record a result that the next watch can understand.

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:21.404
Voltage is the difference in electrical potential between two points.

00:00:21.624 --> 00:00:25.571
In this course, its symbol is the letter U, and its unit is the volt.

00:00:25.791 --> 00:00:29.951
The meter compares the point at the red probe with the point at the black probe.

00:00:30.171 --> 00:00:33.691
A voltage measurement therefore always needs two measurement points.

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:39.383
Current describes how much electric charge passes a point each second.

00:00:39.603 --> 00:00:42.931
Its symbol is the letter I, and its unit is the ampere.

00:00:43.151 --> 00:00:46.863
To measure current, the current in that branch must pass through the instrument.

00:00:47.083 --> 00:00:52.075
The moving dots show the current direction in our model, not the actual speed of the charges.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.603
Resistance describes opposition to current.

00:00:56.823 --> 00:00:59.895
Its symbol is the letter R, and its unit is the ohm.

00:01:00.115 --> 00:01:02.461
For a resistive load, we use Ohm's law.

00:01:02.681 --> 00:01:07.289
Twelve volts across one hundred and twenty ohms gives zero point one amperes.

00:01:07.509 --> 00:01:10.197
Here, we treat the lamp as a constant resistance.

00:01:10.417 --> 00:01:13.425
A real lamp's resistance can change with temperature.

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:20.428
This is a twelve-volt source with protective electrical separation and current limiting.

00:01:20.648 --> 00:01:24.360
F one is the fuse, S one the switch, and H one the cabin lamp.

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:27.161
P one and P two are the lamp terminals.

00:01:27.381 --> 00:01:29.899
The return conductor leads back to the source.

00:01:30.119 --> 00:01:33.831
Practical connections are made during a supervised laboratory session.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.389
The rotary selector chooses the function.

00:01:38.609 --> 00:01:41.553
The display shows the number, unit and measurement mode.

00:01:41.773 --> 00:01:45.805
D C means direct current, and A C means alternating current.

00:01:46.025 --> 00:01:48.116
The black lead goes into COM.

00:01:48.336 --> 00:01:52.432
The red lead goes into volts and ohms, or the appropriate current socket.

00:01:52.652 --> 00:01:57.089
Autoranging selects the range, but you still choose the function and sockets.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:03.245
What are you measuring, between which points, and approximately how much do you expect?

00:02:03.465 --> 00:02:06.431
Inspect the leads, probes and instrument for damage.

00:02:06.651 --> 00:02:09.445
Select the correct function, sockets and range.

00:02:09.665 --> 00:02:12.417
Check the connections before switching on the supply.

00:02:12.637 --> 00:02:17.160
In our training circuit, always disconnect the supply before changing connections.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:20.877
We want to measure across the lamp.

00:02:21.097 --> 00:02:24.660
With the supply disconnected, Saga selects D C volts.

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:27.333
The black lead connects COM to P two.

00:02:27.553 --> 00:02:30.348
The red lead connects the voltage socket to P one.

00:02:30.568 --> 00:02:32.915
The lamp's current path remains complete.

00:02:33.135 --> 00:02:35.716
The meter is connected in parallel with the lamp.

00:02:35.936 --> 00:02:38.539
COM does not automatically mean earth.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:43.828
After checking the connections, we switch on the training circuit.

00:02:44.048 --> 00:02:47.141
The model reads twelve point zero zero volts D C.

00:02:47.361 --> 00:02:52.012
The voltmeter has a high input resistance, so only a small current flows through it.

00:02:52.232 --> 00:02:55.880
Later, we will see why even that small current can affect the result.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:01.328
First, we disconnect the supply and swap the probe positions.

00:03:01.548 --> 00:03:04.108
After checking, we switch the supply on again.

00:03:04.328 --> 00:03:06.760
The meter now reads minus twelve volts.

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:08.943
The magnitude is unchanged.

00:03:09.163 --> 00:03:13.771
The minus sign tells us that the red probe is at a lower potential than the black probe.

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:17.697
Now we want to measure the lamp current.

00:03:17.917 --> 00:03:21.672
Disconnect the supply and open the link between K one and K two.

00:03:21.892 --> 00:03:27.311
Select D C amperes and a current socket with suitable limits, as specified in the manual.

00:03:27.531 --> 00:03:30.688
Connect red to K one, and black COM to K two.

00:03:30.908 --> 00:03:32.657
The meter bridges the gap.

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:38.212
After the instructor has checked the connections, switch on the supply.

00:03:38.432 --> 00:03:41.611
All the current in the lamp branch now passes through the meter.

00:03:41.831 --> 00:03:46.737
Our simplified model reads zero point one amperes, or one hundred milliamperes.

00:03:46.957 --> 00:03:49.261
Record the actual reading with its unit.

00:03:49.481 --> 00:03:53.535
Observe the instrument's fuse requirements, time limit and current limit.

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:58.145
After measuring current, disconnect the supply.

00:03:58.365 --> 00:04:01.267
Remove the meter and restore the link in the circuit.

00:04:01.487 --> 00:04:04.260
Move the red lead back to the volts and ohms socket.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:09.685
This habit reduces the risk of beginning the next voltage measurement with a dangerous connection.

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:13.527
The Gull wants to measure voltage.

00:04:13.747 --> 00:04:17.715
The selector is correct, but the red lead is still in the ampere socket.

00:04:17.935 --> 00:04:19.321
What must change?

00:04:19.541 --> 00:04:21.355
Pause the film and decide.

00:04:21.575 --> 00:04:25.244
The current input has low resistance and can short-circuit a source.

00:04:25.464 --> 00:04:29.368
Simply turning the selector to volts does not make the wrong socket safe.

00:04:29.588 --> 00:04:31.785
Do not try this incorrect connection.

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:39.511
For resistance measurements, the meter supplies its own small test signal.

00:04:39.731 --> 00:04:45.043
Disconnect the supply, verify that voltage is absent, and deal safely with stored energy.

00:04:45.263 --> 00:04:48.591
Isolate the component by disconnecting at least one terminal.

00:04:48.811 --> 00:04:55.680
With the leads in COM and volts and ohms, and the selector on ohms, we measure one thousand ohms: one kilohm.

00:04:57.500 --> 00:05:01.767
With the equipment isolated and verified dead, select continuity.

00:05:01.987 --> 00:05:04.163
First, touch the probe tips together.

00:05:04.383 --> 00:05:06.985
Low resistance normally produces a beep.

00:05:07.205 --> 00:05:11.237
An intact fuse, removed from the circuit, also has low resistance.

00:05:11.457 --> 00:05:13.612
An open path may display O L.

00:05:13.832 --> 00:05:16.477
This can also mean resistance above the range.

00:05:16.697 --> 00:05:19.151
A beep never proves that a circuit is dead.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:25.971
Two one-thousand-ohm resistors are connected in parallel, with no external supply.

00:05:26.191 --> 00:05:30.585
The meter's test current has two paths, and the meter reads five hundred ohms.

00:05:30.805 --> 00:05:35.797
Disconnecting one branch leaves a single path, and the reading becomes one thousand ohms.

00:05:36.017 --> 00:05:40.220
Other connected paths can therefore distort a component's resistance measurement.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:46.843
A transient is a very brief voltage spike, for example when a motor is switched off.

00:05:47.063 --> 00:05:52.801
If insulation breaks down, the supply can continue feeding a short circuit and a dangerous arc.

00:05:53.021 --> 00:05:59.037
The CAT rating describes the measurement environment for which the instrument's protection has been tested.

00:05:59.257 --> 00:06:02.671
The same operating voltage can require different categories.

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:08.361
Category two includes plug-connected appliances on the load side.

00:06:08.581 --> 00:06:15.387
Category three covers fixed installations, distribution boards and fixed motors, including single-phase systems.

00:06:15.607 --> 00:06:19.425
Category four covers the origin of the low-voltage installation.

00:06:19.645 --> 00:06:21.907
It does not mean every outdoor wire.

00:06:22.127 --> 00:06:26.628
The older category one refers to circuits without a direct mains connection.

00:06:26.848 --> 00:06:29.195
Check their particular limits in the manual.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:35.779
Can a category two meter have a higher voltage rating than a category four meter?

00:06:35.999 --> 00:06:36.916
Yes.

00:06:37.136 --> 00:06:41.125
But a higher voltage rating never substitutes for the required category.

00:06:41.345 --> 00:06:43.735
One instrument can have several ratings.

00:06:43.955 --> 00:06:48.285
A higher category does not automatically require a lower rated voltage.

00:06:48.505 --> 00:06:53.497
Check voltage to earth, voltage between sockets, and the limits of the selected function.

00:06:55.500 --> 00:07:03.991
In this example, category two at one thousand volts and category three at six hundred volts are tested with brief six-thousand-volt impulses.

00:07:04.211 --> 00:07:08.285
But the test-source resistances are twelve ohms and two ohms respectively.

00:07:08.505 --> 00:07:10.937
Lower resistance can deliver more current.

00:07:11.157 --> 00:07:15.403
Category four at six hundred volts is tested here at eight thousand volts.

00:07:15.623 --> 00:07:19.889
These are test values, never permissible continuous measuring voltages.

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:25.477
The work plan requires category three, six hundred volts.

00:07:25.697 --> 00:07:28.577
Meter A is category two, one thousand volts.

00:07:28.797 --> 00:07:31.784
Meter B is category three, six hundred volts.

00:07:32.004 --> 00:07:33.391
Which do you choose?

00:07:33.611 --> 00:07:35.467
B meets both requirements.

00:07:35.687 --> 00:07:38.033
The entire measuring system must meet them.

00:07:38.253 --> 00:07:41.965
A category four meter with category two probes is not sufficient.

00:07:42.185 --> 00:07:45.236
Removing probe-tip covers may also change the rating.

00:07:45.456 --> 00:07:46.779
Read the manual.

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:52.773
Resolution is the smallest step on the display.

00:07:52.993 --> 00:07:59.607
Here, the difference between twelve point zero zero and twelve point zero one volts is one hundredth of a volt.

00:07:59.827 --> 00:08:04.691
The error limit describes the instrument's specified deviation under stated conditions.

00:08:04.911 --> 00:08:08.516
Tolerance describes the range allowed for the equipment being tested.

00:08:08.736 --> 00:08:10.848
Keep these three concepts separate.

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:18.803
Assume a specification of plus or minus zero point five per cent of the reading, plus two counts.

00:08:19.023 --> 00:08:21.839
One count is zero point zero one volts.

00:08:22.059 --> 00:08:26.581
At twelve volts, the percentage contribution is zero point zero six volts.

00:08:26.801 --> 00:08:30.065
The two counts contribute zero point zero two volts.

00:08:30.285 --> 00:08:34.104
Adding them gives plus or minus zero point zero eight volts.

00:08:34.324 --> 00:08:37.631
This interval covers only the stated instrument error limit.

00:08:39.500 --> 00:08:44.727
Use the same specification and resolution, but now the display reads twenty-four volts.

00:08:44.947 --> 00:08:46.845
Pause and calculate first.

00:08:47.065 --> 00:08:50.564
The percentage contribution is zero point one two volts.

00:08:50.784 --> 00:08:54.133
Two counts still contribute zero point zero two volts.

00:08:54.353 --> 00:08:58.087
The error limit is plus or minus zero point one four volts.

00:08:58.307 --> 00:09:03.171
The interval runs from twenty-three point eight six to twenty-four point one four volts.

00:09:06.500 --> 00:09:09.572
In the extension example, the meter reads eight volts.

00:09:09.792 --> 00:09:16.640
Its specification is plus or minus one per cent, plus three counts, with a resolution of one hundredth of a volt.

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:21.639
The contributions are zero point zero eight and zero point zero three volts.

00:09:21.859 --> 00:09:24.355
Their sum is zero point one one volts.

00:09:24.575 --> 00:09:27.540
Always use the specification for the actual range.

00:09:29.500 --> 00:09:33.255
The equipment requires twelve volts, plus or minus five per cent.

00:09:33.475 --> 00:09:40.877
Five per cent is zero point six volts, giving an allowed range of eleven point four zero to twelve point six zero volts.

00:09:41.097 --> 00:09:46.516
Our measurement is eleven point five zero, plus or minus zero point one zero volts.

00:09:46.736 --> 00:09:48.421
The whole interval fits.

00:09:48.641 --> 00:09:51.351
This exercise includes the boundary values.

00:09:51.571 --> 00:09:54.771
In real work, acceptance rules must be agreed beforehand.

00:09:56.500 --> 00:09:59.807
Writing 'the voltage is eleven point eight' is not enough.

00:10:00.027 --> 00:10:03.269
Record the unit, measurement points and operating state.

00:10:03.489 --> 00:10:08.481
Identify the instrument, function, range, resolution and relevant specification.

00:10:08.701 --> 00:10:13.501
Include the error limit, requirement and conclusion, together with the date and operator.

00:10:13.721 --> 00:10:15.769
This is fictional teaching data.

00:10:15.989 --> 00:10:19.573
Record real information in actual work; never guess it.

00:10:21.500 --> 00:10:24.209
We now leave the lamp and examine a sensor model.

00:10:24.429 --> 00:10:28.312
Two resistors, each one megohm, divide ten volts equally.

00:10:28.532 --> 00:10:33.012
With no meter connected, the voltage across the lower resistor is five volts.

00:10:33.232 --> 00:10:36.005
What happens when a real voltmeter is connected?

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:42.459
The voltmeter's ten-megohm input is in parallel with the lower resistor.

00:10:42.679 --> 00:10:47.820
The equivalent parallel resistance is about zero point nine zero nine megohms.

00:10:48.040 --> 00:10:53.139
The voltage-divider equation then gives approximately four point seven six two volts.

00:10:53.359 --> 00:10:57.519
With a resolution of one hundredth, the display reads four point seven six.

00:10:57.739 --> 00:11:01.045
The meter has changed the voltage we wanted to investigate.

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:06.413
Change only the meter's input resistance to one megohm.

00:11:06.633 --> 00:11:10.580
One megohm in parallel with one megohm is half a megohm.

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:17.115
Ten times one half, divided by one plus one half, gives approximately three point three three three volts.

00:11:17.335 --> 00:11:19.361
Pause and check the calculation.

00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:26.691
Compare the result with the original five volts, without the meter.

00:11:26.911 --> 00:11:32.329
Divide the difference by the original value to obtain about minus thirty-three point three per cent.

00:11:32.549 --> 00:11:34.853
The minus sign means a lower voltage.

00:11:35.073 --> 00:11:38.529
Large resistances in the sensor circuit make the effect clear.

00:11:38.749 --> 00:11:41.992
Higher input resistance causes less loading in this model.

00:11:42.212 --> 00:11:45.433
Loading error is separate from the instrument's own error limit.

00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:53.111
For the extension example, the source is nine volts and all three resistances are two megohms.

00:11:53.331 --> 00:11:56.595
Without the meter, the voltage is four point five volts.

00:11:56.815 --> 00:11:59.844
The lower parallel combination is one megohm.

00:12:00.064 --> 00:12:02.752
With the meter connected, we obtain three volts.

00:12:02.972 --> 00:12:06.556
Once again, the change is minus thirty-three point three per cent.

00:12:08.500 --> 00:12:10.932
An ammeter also changes the circuit.

00:12:11.152 --> 00:12:15.760
Twelve volts across one hundred and twenty ohms gives zero point one amperes.

00:12:15.980 --> 00:12:20.545
If the meter adds one ohm, the total becomes one hundred and twenty-one ohms.

00:12:20.765 --> 00:12:24.541
Current falls to about zero point zero nine nine two amperes.

00:12:24.761 --> 00:12:27.172
The shunt and fuse cause a voltage drop.

00:12:27.392 --> 00:12:30.400
Instrument manuals often call this burden voltage.

00:12:32.500 --> 00:12:35.657
Why do we measure volts in parallel and amperes in series?

00:12:35.877 --> 00:12:39.077
Why must resistance be measured in an isolated circuit?

00:12:39.297 --> 00:12:41.132
Explain using a diagram.

00:12:41.352 --> 00:12:44.957
Distinguish resolution, error limit, tolerance and loading error.

00:12:45.177 --> 00:12:52.409
The correct category and voltage rating provide protection for the measurement environment, but they do not make the reading more accurate.

00:12:54.500 --> 00:12:57.401
Saga and the Gull now have a method instead of a guess.

00:12:57.621 --> 00:13:01.995
Open the Multimeter Lab, predict the value, and select the function and sockets.

00:13:02.215 --> 00:13:03.964
Connect, read and explain.

00:13:04.184 --> 00:13:07.683
Then draw the correct measurement circuit and write a short record.

00:13:07.903 --> 00:13:13.727
Follow the course submission instructions and the instructor's directions during practical laboratory work.

00:13:15.500 --> 00:13:19.745
This film complements the presentation on multimeters and measurement errors.

00:13:19.965 --> 00:13:25.277
References and additional Swedish videos are listed in the presentation and below this player.

00:13:25.497 --> 00:13:28.655
The instrument shown here is a simplified teaching model.

00:13:28.875 --> 00:13:34.059
For practical work, follow the actual instrument manual and the relevant safety procedures.

00:13:34.279 --> 00:13:36.199
Thank you for joining us aboard.
